=== Second round results by region ===

The second round resulted in a landslide victory for [[Volodymyr Zelenskyy]], who received 73.22% of the vote against 24.45% for incumbent [[President of Ukraine|president]] [[Petro Poroshenko]]. 

Zelenskyy won every oblast except [[Lviv Oblast|Lviv]], while Poroshenko also won among overseas voters. Zelenskyy recorded his strongest result in Luhansk Oblast, with 89.44%, and received more than 85% in [[Dnipropetrovsk Oblast|Dnipropetrovsk]], [[Donetsk Oblast|Donetsk]], [[Odesa Oblast|Odesa]], [[Kharkiv Oblast|Kharkiv]] and [[Zaporizhzhia Oblast|Zaporizhzhia]] [[Oblasts of Ukraine|oblasts]]. By contrast, Poroshenko retained considerably stronger support in [[western Ukraine]], winning 62.79% in Lviv Oblast and receiving over 40% in [[Ternopil Oblast|Ternopil]] and [[Ivano-Frankivsk Oblast|Ivano-Frankivsk]] oblasts.

Although Zelenskyy's support was nationwide, his largest majorities were generally concentrated in the predominantly Russian-speaking east and south. Analysts linked this partly to his more inclusive approach to linguistic and cultural questions and his positioning as an alternative to the established political elite.<ref>{{cite web | title=What Does Volodymyr Zelenskyy's Victory Say About Ukraine? &#124; Chatham House – International Affairs Think Tank | date=22 June 2026 | url=https://www.chathamhouse.org/2019/05/what-does-volodymyr-zelenskyys-victory-say-about-ukraine }}</ref>
 
Poroshenko's stronger performance in western Ukraine was associated with greater support there for his emphasis on the Ukrainian language, the military, closer Western integration and the establishment of an independent Ukrainian Orthodox Church — themes encapsulated in his campaign slogan “Army, Language, Faith”.<ref>{{cite web | title=How Petro Poroshenko Became Ukraine's Top Patriot | date=12 March 2019 | url=https://carnegieendowment.org/europe/posts/2019/03/how-petro-poroshenko-became-ukraines-top-patriot?utm_source=chatgpt.com }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=Making Sense of Ukraine's Complicated, Highly Competitive Presidential Campaign | date=28 March 2019 | url=https://carnegieendowment.org/posts/2019/03/making-sense-of-ukraines-complicated-highly-competitive-presidential-campaign }}</ref>

Nevertheless, Zelenskyy's victory across almost the entire country was seen as a significant weakening of the east–west electoral divide that had characterised previous Ukrainian presidential elections.<ref>{{cite web | title=What Does Zelenskiy's Victory Say About Ukraine? | date=23 April 2019 | url=https://carnegieendowment.org/europe/strategic-europe/2019/04/what-does-zelenskiys-victory-say-about-ukraine }}</ref>

